 "kind": "statement",
 "title": "Stalks of a pullback",
 "statement": "Let $f\\colon Y\\to X$ be a morphism of schemes, let $\\bar y$ be a geometric\npoint of $Y$ and let $\\mathcal F\\in\\shv{\\et X}$. Then\n\n$$\n(f^*\\mathcal F)_{\\bar y}\\cong\\mathcal F_{f\\circ\\bar y},\n$$\n\nnaturally in $\\mathcal F$.",
 "source": {
  "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-proper-base-change/sk-thm-p1/lemma-openclosed-stalk-upperstar.md",
  "place": "node lemma-openclosed-stalk-upperstar, order 1109"
 },
 "stmt_deps": [
  "def-kun-separably-closed"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "The functor $f^*$ is the pullback of sheaves along the morphism of sites\ngiven by base change $\\et X\\to\\et Y$, $V\\mapsto V\\times_XY$. Pulling a point\nof $\\et Y$ back along a morphism of sites gives a point of $\\et X$, and the\nstalk of a pulled-back sheaf at a point is by construction the stalk of the\nsheaf at the transported point. It remains to identify the transport of the\ngeometric point $\\bar y$ along base change with the geometric point\n$f\\circ\\bar y$ of $X$. This is the universal property of the fibre product:\nfor $V\\in\\et X$, lifting $\\bar y$ to $V\\times_XY$ is the same as lifting\n$f\\circ\\bar y$ to $V$, so the two points have the same étale neighbourhoods\nand hence the same stalk functor.",
   "uses": [],
   "elementary": null
  }
 ],
